::GEO_U04_L06_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For an acute angle in a right triangle, which ratio defines sine? {
=A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Sine compares the length of the side opposite the angle to the hypotenuse.
~B. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is cosine, not sine.
~C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This is tangent, not sine.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This is the reciprocal of sine, not the sine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For an acute angle in a right triangle, which ratio defines cosine? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is sine, not cosine.
=B. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Cosine compares the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This is tangent, not cosine.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This reverses the cosine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For an acute angle in a right triangle, which ratio defines tangent? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is sine, not tangent.
~B. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is cosine, not tangent.
=C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#Tangent compares the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This uses the hypotenuse and reverses the sine relationship.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, the opposite side is 8 and the hypotenuse is 17. What is sin A? {
~A. <sup>15</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#This would use the adjacent leg over hypotenuse, which is cosine.
~B. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This is tangent if 15 is adjacent, not sine.
~C. <sup>17</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This reverses the sine ratio.
=D. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse, so sin A = <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, the adjacent leg is 12 and the hypotenuse is 13. What is cos A? {
=A. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, so cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>.
~B. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#This would be sine if 5 is the opposite side.
~C. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This would be tangent if 5 is opposite and 12 is adjacent.
~D. <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This reverses the cosine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, the opposite leg is 9 and the adjacent leg is 12. What is tan A? {
~A. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub>#This reverses the tangent ratio.
=B. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#Tangent is opposite over adjacent, so tan A = <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>.
~C. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This would be sine if 15 is the hypotenuse.
~D. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This would be cosine if 15 is the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which side is never used as the denominator in a tangent ratio? {
~A. The adjacent leg#The adjacent leg is the denominator in tangent.
~B. The opposite leg#The opposite leg is the numerator in tangent.
=C. The hypotenuse#Tangent uses opposite over adjacent, so the hypotenuse is not part of the tangent ratio.
~D. A leg#Both opposite and adjacent are legs used in tangent.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>In a right triangle, why must the reference angle be identified before writing a trig ratio? {
~A. The hypotenuse changes with the angle.#The hypotenuse stays opposite the right angle regardless of the reference angle.
~B. The right angle becomes acute.#The right angle remains 90&deg;; trig ratios use an acute reference angle.
~C. The triangle stops being right.#Choosing a reference angle does not change the triangle type.
=D. Opposite and adjacent sides depend on the chosen acute angle.#Changing the reference angle changes which leg is opposite and which leg is adjacent.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Which memory pattern correctly matches the trig ratios? {
=A. SOH-CAH-TOA#SOH-CAH-TOA stands for sine opposite/hypotenuse, cosine adjacent/hypotenuse, tangent opposite/adjacent.
~B. SAH-COH-TAO#This swaps opposite and adjacent in sine and tangent.
~C. SOA-CAO-TOH#This incorrectly places adjacent or hypotenuse in the denominators.
~D. SSA-AAS-HLH#These are not the sine, cosine, and tangent ratio definitions.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>A student writes sin A = <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>. What correction is needed? {
~A. Use adjacent over hypotenuse for sine.#That repeats the original mistake and defines cosine.
=B. Use opposite over hypotenuse for sine.#Sine is defined as opposite over hypotenuse, not adjacent over hypotenuse.
~C. Use opposite over adjacent for sine.#Opposite over adjacent defines tangent.
~D. Use hypotenuse over adjacent for sine.#This reverses cosine and does not define sine.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For an acute angle in a right triangle, which ratio defines sine? {
~A. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is cosine, not sine.
~B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This is tangent, not sine.
=C. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Sine compares the length of the side opposite the angle to the hypotenuse.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This is the reciprocal of sine, not the sine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For an acute angle in a right triangle, which ratio defines cosine? {
~A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is sine, not cosine.
~B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This is tangent, not cosine.
~C. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#This reverses the cosine ratio.
=D. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#Cosine compares the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>For an acute angle in a right triangle, which ratio defines tangent? {
=A. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>adjacent</sub>#Tangent compares the opposite leg to the adjacent leg.
~B. <sup>opposite</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is sine, not tangent.
~C. <sup>adjacent</sup>&frasl;<sub>hypotenuse</sub>#This is cosine, not tangent.
~D. <sup>hypotenuse</sup>&frasl;<sub>opposite</sub>#This uses the hypotenuse and reverses the sine relationship.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, the opposite side is 8 and the hypotenuse is 17. What is sin A? {
~A. <sup>15</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#This would use the adjacent leg over hypotenuse, which is cosine.
=B. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>#Sine is opposite over hypotenuse, so sin A = <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>17</sub>.
~C. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This is tangent if 15 is adjacent, not sine.
~D. <sup>17</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This reverses the sine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, the adjacent leg is 12 and the hypotenuse is 13. What is cos A? {
~A. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#This would be sine if 5 is the opposite side.
~B. <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This would be tangent if 5 is opposite and 12 is adjacent.
=C. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>#Cosine is adjacent over hypotenuse, so cos A = <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>13</sub>.
~D. <sup>13</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#This reverses the cosine ratio.
}

::GEO_U04_L06_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L06_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.TRI.4<br><br>Question:<br>Relative to angle A, the opposite leg is 9 and the adjacent leg is 12. What is tan A? {
~A. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub>#This reverses the tangent ratio.
~B. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This would be sine if 15 is the hypotenuse.
~C. <sup>12</sup>&frasl;<sub>15</sub>#This would be cosine if 15 is the hypotenuse.
=D. <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#Tangent is opposite over adjacent, so tan A = <sup>9</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>.
}

